To create a C++ program for the Raspberry Pi 4B that controls GPIO pin 12 using hardware PWM and the pigpio library, you'll need to install the pigpio library first, if you haven't already:

```bash
sudo apt-get update
sudo apt-get install pigpio
```

After the library is installed, you can create a C++ program as follows:

1. Create a new file named `gpio_pwm.cpp` and open it in a text editor.

2. Insert the following code into the file:

```cpp
#include <iostream>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <cstdlib>
#include <pigpio.h>

// Pin number
const int gpioPin = 12;

// PWM frequency and duty cycle
const int pwmFrequency = 800; // in Hz
const int dutyCycle = 25;     // in percentage

int main() {
    // Initialize the pigpio library
    if (gpioInitialise() < 0) {
        std::cerr << "Failed to initialize pigpio library" << std::endl;
        return 1;
    }

    // Set the mode of the GPIO pin as output
    gpioSetMode(gpioPin, PI_OUTPUT);

    // Set the hardware PWM
    gpioHardwarePWM(gpioPin, pwmFrequency, dutyCycle * 10000);

    // Run PWM for a while
    usleep(10000000); // Sleep for 10 seconds

    // Stop the hardware PWM and reset the PWM channel
    gpioHardwarePWM(gpioPin, 0, 0);

    // Terminate the pigpio library
    gpioTerminate();

    return 0;
}
```

3. Save the file and close the text editor.

4. Compile the C++ program using `g++`:

```bash
g++ -o gpio_pwm gpio_pwm.cpp -L /usr/local/lib -lwiringPi -lpthread -lpigpio -lrt
```

5. Run the compiled program with the following command:

```bash
sudo ./gpio_pwm
```

This program initializes the pigpio library, sets the specified GPIO pin to output mode, configures hardware PWM with the given frequency and duty cycle, and runs the PWM for 10 seconds before stopping it.

Please remember to use caution while working with GPIO of Raspberry Pi, and make sure to power down your Raspberry Pi before connecting any wires, and double-check the connections before powering it up again.

